          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Set the refrigerator to 37°F and the freezer to 0°F, then prove it with a thermometer instead of trusting the dial. Those two numbers keep food out of the danger zone with a margin for the door openings and thermostat swings every fridge goes through, without freezing your lettuce or wasting electricity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep a thermometer in both compartments permanently. It catches a fridge drifting warm from a worn seal or dusty coils weeks before the milk tells you, and it is the thing that tells you what to keep and what to throw away after a power cut.
          </p>
